networks. Thus，a careful evaluation on H-bonding patterns is performed to find out the plausible PC pathways.
Although, three CPs exhibit similar supramolecular laminate structures, they are not identical. There are distinct variations in the connectivity of H-bonding networks and the manner in which solvent molecules are incorporated (packing diagram are shown in Figure S7-S9, Sl). In Co-fdc, two coordinated water molecules (Ow1 and Ow2) and one lattice water molecule (Ow5) along with two carboxylate oxygen atoms (O2 and O3) formed a H-bonded loop which further connects (Figure S10, Sl) another lattice water (Ow6)to form 1D H-bonded chain (Figure 1a). Another 4-centered H-boned loop was also observed between two coordinated water and two carboxylate oxygens as depicted in Figure 1b. On the other hand, partially protonated template Hbtc²-along with seven water molecules preferred to form a dense H-bonded network in Co-tri (Figure S11, Sl). A puckered boat shaped H-bonded inter template loop (Figure 2a) was found between two neighboring Hbtc²- units which are fused at O5 and Ow7 (Figure 2b) forming an intra template loop as well. The overall H-bonding network is depicted in Figure S12, Sl. For the case of Co-tetra, among four crystallographically independent carboxylate oxygens, O2 was actively engaged in H-bond formation with three water molecules (Figure S13, Sl). Figure S14-S15 (Sl) describe intra/inter template loop formation and overall H-bonding pattern respectively. All these H-bonding parameters are listed in Sl (Table S1-S3).
In order to demonstrate the stability of pelletized samples under $98\%$ R.H.PXRD patterns were compared which showed good correlations with as-synthesized ones (Figure S1-S3, Sl). All the CPs also retained their crystallinity while exposed to $98\%$ R.H. at $80^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ (Figure S16, Sl) under the condition relevant to the most extreme experimental conduction measurements in this study which is a pivotal character of a proton conductor towards successful implementation in fuel cell technology. Differential scanning calorimetry (DsC) measurement confirms that there are no phase transitions (Figure S17, Sl) which ruled out the possibility of transformation of these CPs into superprotonic phases. Analysis of the FTlR spectra (Figure S18, Sl) of Co-tri revealed the characteristics peak at $1719\ c m^{-1}$ attributed to the protonated-COOH group.[12] On the other hand, absence of such characteristic peaks in Co-fdc, and Co-tetra confirmed complete deprotonations of the templates.[12] Thermogravimetric analysis (Figure S19, Sl) showed that Co-tri is stable until $250^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ whereas Co-fdc, and Co-tetra started decomposing around $200^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$
Proton conduction study was carried out by the AC impedance method using compacted pellets of the powdered samples.The Nyquist plots were obtained at $80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ under different R.H. (Figure S20-S22, Sl). The Nyquist plots displayed a semicircle in the highfrequency component and an inclined tail in the low-frequency component, which is a fingerprint of proton conductor showing two distinct charge transport regimes.[13] The semicircle corresponds to the bulk resistance attributing grain interior contribution, and the tail corresponds to the pile-up of protons at the electrodes.[7c.13] A noticeable decrease of the size  of the semicircle with increasing humidity indicates that the conductivity of all three CPs are highly humidity-dependent and increases with raising humidity. As shown in Figure 3, the proton conductivity of Co-tri is greater than that of Co-tetra followed by Co-fdc, and the obtained PC values are $1.49\times10^{-1}\mathsf{S}\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ $4.15\times10^{-2}\mathsf{S}\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ and $4.85\times10^{-3}\ \mathsf{S}\ \mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ respectively at $80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and $98\%$ R.H. The water sorption isotherms (Figure S23-S25, Sl) showed a gradual uptake for Co-fdc and Co-tetra, whereas a steep uptake at lower humidity for Co-tri was observed. It is presumed that the adsorbed water molecules help the diffusion of protons and serve as mediators between the neighboring carboxylthrough hydrogen bond formation which is responsible for such high conductions with increasing R.H.
To study the temperature effect and ensure meaningful correlation to conduction measurements,we further interrogated them with impedance as a function of temperature.Temperature dependence of the conductivity was measured from 40 to $80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ at $98\%$ R.H. (Figure S26-S28, Sl). The Nyquist plots showed the decreased size of the semicircle when switching temperature from 40 to $80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ ，which displayed a noticeable increase in the conductivity (o)when increasing temperature.
